# Editors and pagers, each opened on a real file.

shot    elvrec
for     elvrec elvprsv vi.elvis input.elvis
cap     elvis's crash-recovery pair: elvprsv preserves an interrupted
cap     session, elvrec brings it back.  Nothing has been preserved on
cap     this disk, so elvrec lists nothing at all and only names the file
cap     when you ask for one by name.  `exrecover' is the same job for the
cap     other vi here, and says what it wants.
run     elvrec
wait    6
run     elvrec /dd/tmp/none
wait    6
run     exrecover
wait    6
run     elvprsv /dd/tmp/none
wait    6

shot    emacs
for     emacs emacs.mm1 umacs
cap     MicroEmacs 4.00 editing a file from the disk, with its own mode
cap     line at the foot.
run     emacs /dd/SYS/motd
wait    10
quit    \030\003
run     emacs.mm1 < /nil
wait    10

shot    hexed
cap     This hex editor cannot start: it writes its work file to /r0, the RAM
cap     disk os9exec has no way to provide, and says so.  Twenty programs
cap     here reach for /r0; DOC/STATUS lists them.  `beav' is the binary
cap     editor that needs nothing, and `hexedit' -- a different program with
cap     a similar name -- works once TERMCAP is set as a capability string.
run     hexed /dd/CMDS/today
wait    6
quit    q

shot    hexedit
cap     HEXPERT V2.4, viewing a module.  Like gnuchess it reads TERMCAP as
cap     the capability string itself, so `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry' comes
cap     first; without it the bare form prints the terminal type and exits.
cap     One 16-byte row and the mode is the whole display -- that is the
cap     program, not a clipped photograph; arrows, `n' and space all leave
cap     it where it is.  Its -d option is separately broken -- `file not
cap     accessible' for a file that is readable, which this disproves.
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     hexedit /dd/CMDS/today
wait    12
kill

shot    pbyte
for     pbyte chbase
cap     pbyte patches bytes in a file at a hex offset -- the smallest
cap     binary editor here.  `chbase' beside it is NOT a module-base
cap     changer whatever DOC/INDEX said: it converts a NUMBER from one
cap     base to another, which its own banner spells out.
run     pbyte
wait    8
run     chbase
wait    8

shot    sed
for     sed sed_1.06 psc
cap     sed doing what a stream editor does, twice over, on the disk's own
cap     documentation.
run     head -n 8 /dd/SYS/holidays > /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    6
run     sed "s/Day/DAY/g" /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    8
run     psc < /nil
wait    8

shot    vi
for     vi view
cap     The EFFO vi -- its source in SRC/effo_vi is the Berkeley ex source
cap     itself, not a clone, which is why it has the name and keeps it.
cap     WHAT YOU SEE HERE IS NOT WHAT ITS USERS SEE.  Under this harness --
cap     a pseudo-terminal rendered as vt100 -- it paints the first line and
cap     a half-drawn rule and no more; on a real terminal it works fine and
cap     is used daily.  Tried and did NOT explain it (2026-08-30): 24 lines
cap     and 36, TERMCAP as a path and as an entry, ^L and ^R.  So this is a
cap     fact about the harness until somebody explains it.  `elvis',
cap     `vi_nocio' and REBUILT/vi_1.0 fill the screen under both, each with
cap     its own card.
run     vi /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    20
send    \033
wait    3
send    1G
wait    10
quit    \033:q!\r
run     view < /nil
wait    10

shot    elvis
for     elvis vi.elvis input.elvis ctags.elvis
cap     elvis, Steve Kirkendall's vi -- the one on this disk that paints a
cap     full screen.  Opened on the collection's own README-CIO, twenty-two
cap     lines of it at once, exactly as vi should.
cap     `vi.elvis', `input.elvis' and `ctags.elvis' travel with it -- the
cap     same editor under the name a Unix hand types, its input helper, and
cap     its tags builder.  All three were credited here and never run until
cap     2026-08-30; they are asked for after elvis has been left.
run     elvis /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r
run     ctags.elvis /dd/SRC/keep/about.c
wait    10
run     ls -l /dd/tags
wait    8
run     input.elvis
wait    8
run     vi.elvis -?
wait    10

shot    pvic
for     vi_1.0
cap     PVIC 1.0, the Portable VI Clone, in CMDS/REBUILT because the name
cap     `vi' was already taken by the EFFO one; `vi_cio' and `vi_nocio' are
cap     the 1.0a builds of the same program.  It is a working vi: tildes down
cap     the left, the file painted a screenful at a time, and o-i-escape-:wq
cap     writes the line you typed.  Asked for by path, since it is not on
cap     the login PATH.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/vi_1.0 /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r

